# ==============================================================================
# SETTINGS FOR RUNNING THE APP ON YOUR COMPUTER
# ==============================================================================
#
# What is this file?
#   The app needs to know which Supabase project (your database and logins) to
#   use. Instead of writing that into the code, the app reads it from a file
#   named .env.local on your computer. This file, .env.example, is a template
#   for .env.local: it shows which settings are needed, with fake values.
#
# What does XXXX mean?
#   A value with XXXX in it is a placeholder: it only shows what your value
#   looks like. It is not a real value, and the app doesn't work until you
#   replace it. Replace the whole value between the quotes with the value you
#   copy from the website. For example, this line:
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://XXXX.supabase.co"
#   becomes something like this in your .env.local:
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://abcdefghijklmnopqrst.supabase.co"
#   And "sb_publishable_XXXX" becomes your whole key, for example
#   "sb_publishable_AbC123...". (The key you copy already starts with
#   sb_publishable_, so don't type that part twice.)
#
# How to use it:
#   1. Make a copy of this file in the same folder, named .env.local
#      (in the terminal: cp .env.example .env.local).
#   2. In .env.local, replace each placeholder value (everything between the
#      quotes) with your own value. Keep the quotes.
#   3. Save .env.local. If the app is running (npm run dev), stop it with
#      Ctrl+C and start it again: the app reads .env.local when it starts.
#
# How to read it:
#   - Each setting is one line: NAME="value". Don't put spaces around the =.
#   - Lines that start with # are notes for people, like this one. The app
#     ignores them.
#
# On your computer vs. online:
#   - .env.local is used only when the app runs on your own computer (for
#     example with npm run dev), while you build it and try it out.
#   - When you put the app online, it runs on Vercel's servers, and Vercel
#     never sees .env.local (the file isn't on GitHub). So you type the same
#     two settings into Vercel's website: your project > Settings >
#     Environment Variables. After you add or change them there, redeploy the
#     app: Vercel builds the values into the app when it builds it.
#   - The Edge Functions (the small programs that run on Supabase) don't use
#     this file either. Their keys are stored on Supabase, with the command
#     npx supabase secrets set.
#
# Is it safe?
#   - .env.local is never uploaded to GitHub: the project's .gitignore file
#     tells Git to skip it. This template IS on GitHub, so it holds only fake
#     values. Never put your real values in this template.
#   - Both settings below are meant to be public: settings whose names start
#     with NEXT_PUBLIC_ are built into the web pages that every visitor's
#     browser downloads. So never put a secret key in .env.local.
#   - The automated tests need more settings, some of them secret. Those go in
#     a different file: see .env.test.example. You don't need it to run the app.
# ==============================================================================

# Your Supabase project's web address. It looks like
# https://abcdefghijklmnopqrst.supabase.co
# Where to find it: in the Supabase dashboard, open your project. The address
# is under the project's name, with a Copy button.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://XXXX.supabase.co"

# Your Supabase project's publishable key. It starts with sb_publishable_
# Where to find it: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API Keys, under
# Publishable key. Click the copy icon next to it: the page shows only the
# start of the key.
# Why does the name say ANON_KEY? Supabase used to call this key the "anon"
# key, and the app's code still uses that name. Your publishable key goes here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="sb_publishable_XXXX"
